This project presents the design and development of a glue-dispensing jig system for the assembly of a novel microchannel polymer heat exchanger (MPHX). The system is intended to automate and standardize the process of applying adhesive to ABS MPHX plates and bonding them to polycarbonate lamina, improving consistency, throughput, and reliability. Key design requirements include achieving throughput rate (1,000 modules per heat exchanger within an 8-hour workday), ensuring strong adhesive bonds capable of withstanding internal pressures exceeding 10 psi, and maintaining precise alignment during assembly.
To meet these requirements, a roller-based adhesive application system was selected due to its ability to uniformly distribute a high-viscosity solvent-based adhesive. The system integrates three primary subsystems: an adhesive roller assembly, a jig system for alignment and handling of plates and lamina, and a feed mechanism utilizing roller bearings to guide the jig through the process. Adjustable pressure rollers ensure consistent contact between the plates and adhesive roller, while modular jig components enable repeatable alignment and efficient stacking during curing.

Experimental validation confirmed that the selected adhesive can withstand pressures up to 50 psi without failure, exceeding design requirements. The proposed system emphasizes simplicity, cleanability, scalability, and adaptability to varying adhesive properties. Overall, the design provides a robust and efficient solution for semi-automated MPHX module assembly with potential for future commercial application.
